The Security Council,

Recalling its previous resolutions, statements of its President, and press statements concerning the Sudan, in particular resolutions 1591 (2005), 1651 (2006), 1665 (2006), 1672 (2006), 1713 (2006), 1779 (2007), 1841 (2008), 1891 (2009), 1945 (2010), 1982 (2011), 2035 (2012), 2091 (2013), 2138 (2014), 2200 (2015), 2265 (2016), 2340 (2017), 2363 (2017), 2400 (2018), 2455 (2019), 2508 (2020), 2562 (2021), 2620 (2022), 2676 (2023), 2724 (2024), 2736 (2024), and 2750 (2024),

Reaffirming its firm commitment to the sovereignty, unity, independence, and territorial integrity of the Sudan,

Gravely concerned by the continued fighting, devastating humanitarian crisis, and acute food insecurity across the Sudan, and in particular the catastrophic situation in Darfur,

Condemning in the strongest terms the continued attacks against civilians, civilian infrastructure, and displacement camps, notably the Abu Shouk camp, as well as the ongoing siege and military escalation in and around El Fasher,

Recalling the obligations of all parties under international humanitarian law and international human rights law to protect civilians and civilian objects, to allow and facilitate the rapid, safe, full, and unhindered passage of humanitarian relief, and welcoming in this regard the continued operation of the Adre border crossing,

Reiterating that the targeted measures and the arms embargo imposed pursuant to resolution 1591 (2005) and subsequent resolutions remain an essential tool to curb destabilizing flows of weapons into Darfur, reminding all Member States of their obligation to strictly comply with these measures, and expressing concern over external support and the recruitment of foreign fighters that fuel the conflict,

Emphasizing the vital importance of the Panel of Experts appointed pursuant to resolution 1591 (2005) in providing independent reporting and monitoring compliance with the sanctions regime,

Determining that the situation in the Sudan continues to constitute a threat to international peace and security,

Acting under Chapter VII of the Charter of the United Nations,

1. Decides to extend until 12 September 2026 the mandate of the Panel of Experts originally appointed pursuant to resolution 1591 (2005) and previously extended by subsequent resolutions, including resolution 2750 (2024), and expresses its intention to review the mandate and take appropriate action regarding further extension no later than 12 August 2026;

2. Requests the Panel of Experts to provide to the Security Council Committee established pursuant to resolution 1591 (2005) concerning the Sudan (hereafter “the Committee”) an interim report on its activities no later than 12 March 2026, and to provide to the Council, after discussion with the Committee, a final report by 13 January 2026 with its findings and recommendations;

3. Requests the Panel of Experts to provide updates to the Committee regarding its activities on a quarterly basis or as requested by the Committee;

4. Urges all parties and all Member States, as well as international, regional, and subregional organizations, to ensure full cooperation with the Panel of Experts and the safety and unhindered access of its members to persons, documents, and sites necessary for the execution of its mandate;

5. Reiterates its demand in resolution 2736 (2024) that the Rapid Support Forces halt the siege of El Fasher, and calls for an immediate cessation of hostilities and de-escalation across Darfur and the wider country;

6. Reminds all Member States of their obligation to strictly enforce the arms embargo measures in Darfur established pursuant to resolution 1591 (2005), and calls upon all Member States to refrain from external interference that fuels the conflict and instability;

7. Expresses its intention to review the benchmarks established pursuant to resolution 2676 (2023) and progress made toward them;

8. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

